Q: D3 simple line graph with hardcoded data A: <p>You don’t need to replace <code>d3.csv()</code> with any other function in this case.</p>
<p><code>d3.csv()</code> is only used when you are loading data from an external CSV file asynchronously. Since your data is already hardcoded as a JavaScript array, you can <strong><a href="https://exabet88id.com/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">simply</a></strong> remove <code>d3.csv()</code> completely and run the code directly.</p>
<h3>Correct approach</h3>
<p>Move everything inside the <code>d3.csv()</code> callback outside of it:</p>
<pre><code>var data = [
  { date: 2008, num: 54248 },
  { date: 2009, num: 54446 },
  { date: 2010, num: 54731 },
  { date: 2011, num: 55212 },
  { date: 2012, num: 55595 },
  { date: 2013, num: 56165 },
  { date: 2014, num: 56690 },
  { date: 2015, num: 56929 },
  { date: 2016, num: 57005 },
  { date: 2017, num: 57010 }
];

// format data
data.forEach(function(d) {
  d.date = +d.date;
  d.num = +d.num;
});

// set scale domains
x.domain(d3.extent(data, d =&gt; d.date));
y.domain([0, d3.max(data, d =&gt; d.num)]);

// draw line
svg.append(&quot;path&quot;)
  .data([data])
  .attr(&quot;class&quot;, &quot;line&quot;)
  .attr(&quot;d&quot;, valueline);

// X axis
svg.append(&quot;g&quot;)
  .attr(&quot;transform&quot;, &quot;translate(0,&quot; + height + &quot;)&quot;)
  .call(d3.axisBottom(x));

// Y axis
svg.append(&quot;g&quot;)
  .call(d3.axisLeft(y));
</code></pre>
<h3>Summary</h3>
<p>There is no replacement function needed. Just remove <code>d3.csv()</code> entirely because your data is already available in memory as a JavaScript array.</p>

Q: How to prevent parent click event in react native? A: <p>In React Native, touch events don’t behave exactly like DOM events, so <code>stopPropagation()</code> is not always reliable depending on which touch components you use.</p>
<p>In your case, the issue happens because <code>TouchableWithoutFeedback</code> (parent) is capturing the touch event before the child handler is properly executed.</p>
<hr />
<h2>✅ Recommended solution</h2>
<h3>1. Avoid nesting conflicting touchables</h3>
<p>Instead of relying on event propagation, restructure touch handling:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;TouchableWithoutFeedback onPress={this.onParentClick}&gt;
  &lt;View&gt;
    &lt;Text&gt;How to prevent parent click event&lt;/Text&gt;

    &lt;TouchableOpacity onPress={this.onChildClick}&gt;
      &lt;Text&gt;Click Me&lt;/Text&gt;
    &lt;/TouchableOpacity&gt;

  &lt;/View&gt;
&lt;/TouchableWithoutFeedback&gt;
</code></pre>
<hr />
<h3>2. Use <code>onStartShouldSetResponder</code> (more reliable)</h3>
<p>This is the React Native way to control touch propagation:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;View onStartShouldSetResponder={() =&gt; true} onTouchEnd={this.onParentClick}&gt;

  &lt;Text&gt;How to prevent parent click event&lt;/Text&gt;

  &lt;TouchableOpacity
    onPress={this.onChildClick}
    onStartShouldSetResponder={() =&gt; true}
  &gt;
    &lt;Text&gt;Click Me&lt;/Text&gt;
  &lt;/TouchableOpacity&gt;

&lt;/View&gt;
</code></pre>
<hr />
<h3>3. Stop propagation properly (limited support)</h3>
<p>If you still want event blocking:</p>
<pre><code>onChildClick = (event) =&gt; {
  event.stopPropagation?.();
  console.log(&quot;Child clicked&quot;);
};
</code></pre>
<p>⚠️ Note: <code>stopPropagation()</code> is not fully reliable in React Native touch system.</p>
<hr />
<h2>🧠 Best practice explanation</h2>
<p>React Native does not use DOM events. Instead it uses a <strong>gesture responder <a href="https://octoplay88slot.com/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">octoplay</a> system</strong>, so:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Parent touchables often “win” gesture priority</p>
</li>
<li><p>Child touchables may not prevent parent triggers</p>
</li>
<li><p>Proper solution is controlling responder behavior, not event bubbling</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>🟢 Conclusion</h2>
<p>To reliably prevent parent click in React Native:</p>
<p>✔ Prefer restructuring touch handlers<br />
✔ Use responder system (<code>onStartShouldSetResponder</code>)<br />
✔ Avoid relying on <code>stopPropagation()</code> alone</p>
